             obvious one. (1) INLET/OUTLET: section 3.4.2 implements a full
             InletOutletManager with ghost particles, interpolation equations, a
             stepper and a fluid-to-inlet/outlet conversion callback, and section 4.3
             exercises it on flow past a cylinder at Re 200, reporting lift 1.524 and
             drag 0.722 within 5 percent of the reference and Strouhal 0.2 within
             2 percent. That is a working, quantitatively checked answer to the open
             outlet problem in register item 32. (2) BUILD MODEL: pure Python with
             runtime code generation through compyle to Cython, OpenMP, OpenCL and
             CUDA, so there are no architecture-specific static libraries of the kind
             note L-8 used to rule out DualSPHysics on aarch64. The paper does NOT
             test ARM, so this is an absence of the known blocker, not a positive
             port report. (3) TWO-CODE TIMING: table 2 gives PySPH v1.0b1 against
             DualSPHysics v5.0 on the same i5-7400, 329.55 s versus 146.63 s single
             core and 90.82 s versus 41.26 s on four OpenMP cores, so roughly 2.2x
             slower, which is a rare like-for-like number between two particle codes.
             Also: PySPH and PHANTOM are the only packages in the paper's own table 1
             with public CI, and around 900 tests run on Linux and Windows.}
}
